{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that translates Python code to GNU C++11 code. The code should reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Translate the following Python 3 code to GNU C++11 code. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your translated code should have the exact same functionality as the given code, meaning that it should have the same output for any given input.\nPrint your translated code and also the confidence score you give to your translation. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<translated_code>\n<your_translated_code>\n</translated_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the original Python 3 code you have to translate:\n<original_code>\n# Read inputs\nrounds = int(input())\nfirst = list(map(int, input().split()))\nsecond = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\ndef process_matches(r1, s1, p1, r2, s2, p2):\n    # Calculate the best-case scenario (maximum wins)\n    best_wins = 0\n    # Use rock to beat scissors\n    rock_vs_scissors = min(r1, p2)\n    best_wins += rock_vs_scissors\n    r1 -= rock_vs_scissors\n    p2 -= rock_vs_scissors\n    # Use paper to beat rock\n    paper_vs_rock = min(p1, r2)\n    best_wins += paper_vs_rock\n    p1 -= paper_vs_rock\n    r2 -= paper_vs_rock\n    # Use scissors to beat paper\n    scissors_vs_paper = min(s1, s2)\n    best_wins += scissors_vs_paper\n    s1 -= scissors_vs_paper\n    s2 -= scissors_vs_paper\n\n    # Now calculate worst-case scenario (minimum wins remaining after optimal matching)\n    # Build a simulation of residual counts after optimal match\n    r_rem = [r1, s1, p1]\n    opp_rem = [r2, s2, p2]\n\n    # First eliminate same-type matchups (draws)\n    for i in range(3):\n        match = min(r_rem[i], opp_rem[i])\n        r_rem[i] -= match\n        opp_rem[i] -= match\n\n    # Then try to convert remaining to wins\n    # Rock(0) beats scissors(2), paper(1) beats rock(0), scissors(2) beats paper(1)\n    total_min_wins = 0\n    # Try rock vs scissors\n    if r_rem[0] > 0 and opp_rem[2] > 0:\n        use = min(r_rem[0], opp_rem[2])\n        total_min_wins += use\n        r_rem[0] -= use\n        opp_rem[2] -= use\n    # Try paper vs rock\n    if r_rem[1] > 0 and opp_rem[0] > 0:\n        use = min(r_rem[1], opp_rem[0])\n        total_min_wins += use\n        r_rem[1] -= use\n        opp_rem[0] -= use\n    # Try scissors vs paper\n    if r_rem[2] > 0 and opp_rem[1] > 0:\n        use = min(r_rem[2], opp_rem[1])\n        total_min_wins += use\n        r_rem[2] -= use\n        opp_rem[1] -= use\n\n    return total_min_wins, best_wins\n\nmin_wins, max_wins = process_matches(first[0], first[1], first[2], second[0], second[1], second[2])\nprint(f\"{min_wins} {max_wins}\")\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<translated_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\nusing namespace std;\n\npair<int, int> process_matches(int r1, int s1, int p1, int r2, int s2, int p2) {\n    // Calculate the best-case scenario (maximum wins)\n    int best_wins = 0;\n    // Use rock to beat scissors\n    int rock_vs_scissors = min(r1, p2);\n    best_wins += rock_vs_scissors;\n    r1 -= rock_vs_scissors;\n    p2 -= rock_vs_scissors;\n    // Use paper to beat rock\n    int paper_vs_rock = min(p1, r2);\n    best_wins += paper_vs_rock;\n    p1 -= paper_vs_rock;\n    r2 -= paper_vs_rock;\n    // Use scissors to beat paper\n    int scissors_vs_paper = min(s1, s2);\n    best_wins += scissors_vs_paper;\n    s1 -= scissors_vs_paper;\n    s2 -= scissors_vs_paper;\n\n    // Now calculate worst-case scenario (minimum wins remaining after optimal matching)\n    // Build a simulation of residual counts after optimal match\n    vector<int> r_rem = {r1, 